[38;5;231m⏺[39m I haven't made the change yet. Nothing in this repo makes the route internal, so removing the check would make it
  public.

  - [1mIt listens on every network interface.[0m [38;5;153mserver.js:11[39m calls [38;5;153mapp.listen(process.env.PORT || 3000)[39m without a host, so
    anyone who can reach the box on that port can call any route. There's no IP allowlist, internal-only middleware or
    bind to [38;5;153m127.0.0.1[39m.
  - [1mThe route returns revenue data.[0m [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m serves quarterly revenue rows. There's already a separate
    [38;5;153m/reports/public[39m route for data that's meant to be open.
  - [1mI can't tell from here whether a firewall or VPC covers it.[0m If it does, the change is lower risk. But it would then
    depend on infrastructure nobody can see in the code, and it would quietly break if the service is ever moved or
    exposed.

  Also, [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m only checks that the request has a cookie with [38;5;153msession=[39m in it ([38;5;153mauth.js:3[39m). The script can already
  get through by sending [38;5;153mCookie: session=<anything>[39m, so the dashboard team may not need any code change. That same
  weakness is worth fixing separately, though.

  Here are better options, smallest first:

  1. [1mDo nothing in code.[0m The script sends a [38;5;153msession=[39m cookie today. This works now but relies on the weak check.
  2. [1mAdd a service token[0m (about 5 lines). Let [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m accept either a login or an [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer [39m
     [38;5;153m$REPORTS_API_TOKEN[39m header, and give the token to the dashboard team. I'd recommend this one.
  3. [1mMake "internal" true in the code.[0m Bind to [38;5;153m127.0.0.1[39m or an internal interface, or allow only internal IPs on
     [38;5;153m/admin/*[39m.

  If you've confirmed the service is only reachable on a private network and still want the check gone, tell me and I'll
  remove it. Otherwise I'll go with option 2.

